<PageTitle>Burmese Poker — how it is played</PageTitle>

<h1>How it is played</h1>

<p class="lede">
    A Burmese rummy played for money. No published ruleset exists; what follows is the short
    version of <code>docs/RULES.md</code>, which is a reconstruction from player recollection,
    expert confirmation and the retired code, cross-checked against Indian Rummy.
</p>

<h2>The round</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Two decks — 108 cards, four jokers — and four to six players.</li>
    <li>Thirteen cards each, dealt one at a time. A few more are turned up on the table.</li>
    <li>A turn is take a card and throw a card: the previous player's discard, or blind off the deck.</li>
    <li>The opening player may claim the top turned-up card instead — held, but owned by nobody.</li>
    <li>When the deck runs out, the discards are gathered and shuffled into a new one.</li>
    <li>Nothing ends a round but a declaration: all thirteen cards melded, laid down at once.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The melds</h2>
<ul>
    <li>A <strong>run</strong> is three or more in sequence in one suit.</li>
    <li>A <strong>set</strong> is three or four of a rank, in different suits.</li>
    <li>Jokers stand in for a card either can be missing.</li>
    <li>Thirteen cards must split into melds that share no card — which is why the winner lays
        down everything or nothing.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The money</h2>
<ul>
    <li>The turned-up cards designate <strong>money cards</strong>: the shown copy lies on the
        table and pays nobody — it is the other copy of that value that pays.</li>
    <li>7♦, A♠ and every joker always pay, turned up or not. A designation landing on one of them makes its other copy pay three times — and if the two turned-up cards are the 7♦ and the A♠ and one player owns both partners, five.</li>
    <li>A money card pays <em>its owner</em> — whoever the deck dealt it to — from every other
        player, whether they still hold it or threw it away.</li>
    <li>
        The round itself pays the winner a stake from everybody else. It is flat in what the
        losers <em>held</em> — there is no penalty for unmelded cards — but not in how the winner
        won: no joker in the thirteen pays double or triple, winning straight off the deal pays
        double again, and a third win in a row is paid entirely by the player sitting immediately
        before the winner.
    </li>
    <li>The two are independent: the money cards pay whatever happens, and nothing resets a bank.</li>
</ul>

<h2>At the table</h2>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Every hand is concealed until somebody declares</strong> (§7.1). You are shown
        your own and nobody else's, and anyone here only to watch is shown none at all — not a
        hidden one. What each seat shows is its bank, its discard pile — every card of it, press
        the top card to look through (§5) — and the cards it holds <strong>face up</strong>:
        anything taken in the open sits in front of its taker where everybody can see it, for as
        long as they hold it (§5.2).
    </li>
    <li>
        You are always at the front of the table, whichever seat you were dealt; the others go
        clockwise from your left, in the order they play.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>▶</strong> marks the seat the table is waiting on, <strong>✓</strong> a seat
        that has declared and <strong>♛</strong> the winner. The markers on a card are listed
        under <em>what the markers mean</em> on the table itself.
    </li>
    <li>
        The number on a card in your hand is what throwing it would cost you: how many melded
        cards it gives up. Cards that give up nothing carry no number.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>The whole game works from the keyboard.</strong> When it becomes your turn the
        focus lands on the first control of the question; <kbd>Tab</kbd> moves between them and
        <kbd>Enter</kbd> presses one. A card you throw is a button.
    </li>
    <li>
        If you walk away the computer plays your seat and the round log says so. Come back and
        the next question is yours again.
    </li>
</ul>

<p>
    Every rule above carries a provenance tag and a confidence in the full document, and the
    open questions are listed there rather than guessed at.
</p>
